\chapter*{Abstract}\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{Abstract}
Spain has experienced a sustained decline in fertility alongside a
marked postponement of childbearing, with an increasing share of births
occurring at older maternal ages. In this context, elective oocyte
cryopreservation (OC) has emerged as a proposed policy instrument to mitigate
the biological constraints associated with delayed motherhood. Recent
political proposals to publicly fund OC raise the question of whether
such an intervention could increase fertility and contribute to
long-term fiscal sustainability. Yet evidence on its aggregate
demographic effectiveness remains limited.

The paper analyses demographic and fiscal implications of publicly financed elective OC in a persistently low-fertility economy. Drawing annual Spanish data sets from 1976–2022, I employ an Interrupted Time Series (ITS) design to recover the structural alterations in fertility dynamics based on an endogenously defined breakpoint. The estimates indicate a large and statistically significant negative level shift of adjusted total fertility at the breakpoint with no subsequent convergence towards the pre-trend counterfactual. Age-specific differences reveal a sharp asymmetry: fertility at 35–39 demonstrates partial recovery consistent with postponement, while fertility at 40–44 is permanently below its counterfactual trajectory. This pattern does not conform with complete intertemporal substitution and suggests that delayed childbearing does not lead to improved total fertility levels at the aggregate level. 

I embed these demographic constraints in a decision-analytic cost framework that incorporates utilisation behaviour, age-dependent success probabilities, and a demographic additionality parameter. Assuming empirically reliable parameter values, the marginal cost per additional live birth is much greater than the standard rate requirements established in policy literature and does not fulfil a generational accounting-derived fiscal self-sustainability condition. Although assisted reproduction may provide positive lifetime net fiscal contributions to families in terms of positive lifetime contributions at the individual level, such returns do not aggregate in the absence of any fertility additionality. 

The findings suggest a near correspondence between postponement-regulated reproductive technologies and aggregate fertility outcomes: policies that relax biological timing constraints in the absence of changing underlying fertility preferences are unlikely to counter structural low fertility or achieve equilibrium in fiscal settings. The salient issue of publicly funded OC is therefore redistribution and access in the interest of policy and not a pro-natalist mechanism to be employed.

\textbf{Keywords}: Elective oocyte cryopreservation; Fertility
postponement; Health policy in Spain
