\chapter{Introduction}\label{chap:introduction}
In his 1968 book The Population Bomb, scientist Paul Ehrlich predicted
that ''hundreds of millions'' will soon starve to death due to human
population growth. He supported stringent birth control, ''by compulsion
if voluntary methods fail'', after experimenting with the concept of
''interstellar transport for surplus people'' \citep{economist2025}. It
could not have been more wrong. The fertility rate, the number of
children a woman might anticipate having in her lifetime, has been
falling, despite the fact that the population is continuously growing.
Furthermore, two-thirds of the world' s population today
resides in nations where the replacement rate of 2.1, the conventional
estimate of what is required to maintain a steady population, is below
\citep{worldbank2025}.

318,005 births occurred in Spain in 2024, the lowest number since the
statistics series started in 1941, according to the most recent data
from Spain's National Statistics Institute \citep{ine2025mnp}. Additionally, this
figure shows a decline of almost 25\% over the previous ten years
\citep{funcas2024}.

Historical comparisons also show the delay in first pregnancies. Women
gave birth to their first child at an average age of 32.6 years now,
compared to 25.6 years in 1980 and 33.2 years if only Spanish women are
analysed \citep{funcas2024}.

Against this demographic backdrop, assisted reproductive technologies
(ART) have increasingly been perceived as a means to mitigate the
biological constraints associated with delayed childbearing. The use of
elective OC has expanded rapidly in Spain over
the last decade. According to data from the National Activity Registry
of the Spanish Fertility Society \citep{sef2024registro}, the number of women undergoing
egg freezing increased almost eighteenfold between 2011 and 2021. This
trend has coincided with a pronounced postponement of childbearing:
Spain records the highest share of births to mothers aged 40 and over in
the European Union, accounting for 10.7\% of all live births in 2021
\citep{blas2025}.

The Popular Party' s, Spain's conservatives, policy
statement approved at the July congress includes for the first time
public subsidies for egg freezing for women who wish to delay
motherhood. Until now, Social Security has only covered this service in
cases of illnesses that damage fertility, but not for the mere desire to
postpone it, and women have had to resort to private clinics \citep{blas2025}.

`Thousands of Spaniards are unable to start a family because economic,
political and even social circumstances prevent them from doing so,'
according to the PP. The PP is therefore committed to `implementing
measures that enable people to have the number of children they want,
when they want, and that facilitate personal and professional progress,
such as egg freezing for young women,' the policy paper stresses. `We
believe in a support system capable of taking into account the specific
needs of each family --- from single-parent families to large families
--- offering flexible, fair responses that respect their freedom and way
of life' \citep{partidopopular2025}.

The project is modelled after the Galician government' s
program which provides publicly funded OC for women aged 30--35. This
benefit is about to be incorporated into the regional public health
system by the Galician project under the reproductive health criteria
\citep{xunta2024}.

Existing studies assess OC either from a clinical perspective or under
individual-level economic models, but evidence remains scarce on its
cost-effectiveness and fiscal sustainability when implemented as a
publicly funded policy and evaluated at the population level.

Public funding for OC is limited to clearly specified medical
indications in the majority of European health systems, such as
fertility preservation prior to gonadotoxic therapy; elective or
age-related OC is specifically not reimbursed. There are significant
socioeconomic disparities in access since women seeking elective OC in
these settings are forced to rely almost entirely on private providers.
Sweden is a different but still constrained example, where elective OC
is readily accessible and legally allowed through private clinics, and
where the technique is well accepted in society \citep{gambadauro2023}.

This paper has three objectives. First, determine whether publicly
funded access to elective OC is likely to increase
fertility outcomes in the medium term. Second, evaluate whether such a
policy can be self-financing in the long run, in the sense that any
additional births generated by the intervention result in an increase in
aggregate output that is at least comparable to its overall cost. This
entails, on the one hand, assessing the fiscal feasibility of a proposed
policy measure advanced within the national political debate, namely
whether it can be financed under current budgetary constraints and
sustained over time. On the other, examine whether incorporating OC into
the publicly funded health benefits package is likely to affect
reproductive behaviour and completed fertility, that is, whether
expanded access translates into higher birth rates rather than merely
shifting the timing or mode of conception. Third, conduct an economic
evaluation of the proposed programme: constructing an incremental
cost-effectiveness ratio (ICER) relative to the status quo of no public
subsidy, estimating the annual budget impact under plausible uptake
scenarios, and analysing the distributional consequences of universal
versus means-tested versus co-payment designs using concentration-curve
methodology.

The remainder of the paper is structured as follows. Chapter~\ref{chap:literature} reviews the relevant literature. Chapter~\ref{chap:methodology} outlines the methodological framework and identification strategy. Chapter~\ref{chap:demographic} presents the demographic results, while Chapter~\ref{chap:economic} develops the economic evaluation. Chapter~\ref{chap:discussion} concludes. The full econometric specification, supplementary estimation output, and diagnostic material are reported in Annex~\ref{app:model}.


\chapter{Literature review}\label{chap:literature}
This section reviews the literature most closely related to the research
question of this thesis: whether public funding for elective OC can be
justified as a cost-effective and fiscally sustainable policy to support
fertility in Spain.

The literature' s main conclusion is that access to ART
is extremely uneven and strongly correlated with socioeconomic position.
\citet{alonpinilla2021} demonstrate that access to ART and the decision
between public and private provision are significantly influenced by
income and education using microdata from the Spanish \emph{Encuesta de
Fecundidad}. Treatment in private clinics is substantially more common
for women with greater incomes and levels of education. Women from
poorer socioeconomic backgrounds, on the other hand, are more likely to
be denied access to ART, even when they report reproductive issues
(2021:9). Results vary per sector as well. Compared to women treated in
the public sector or in both sectors, those treated alone in the private
sector have a greater cumulative pregnancy incidence (2021:15). From a
policy standpoint, these trends indicate a distinct selection mechanism
via which institutional design and financial limitations influence both
access and reproductive results.

Similar dynamics are observed at the European level. Significant
cross-national diversity in eligibility requirements, age restrictions,
and public coverage is documented by \citet{seiz2023}, who also
demonstrate how these institutional disparities consistently impact
access to ART, with particularly negative impacts for women from lower
socioeconomic backgrounds (2023:3). All of the evidence points to price
as a major factor in the wide margin of access. It also draws attention
to a significant drawback of subsidization strategies. If waiting lists,
age restrictions, or clinical capacity limitations are still in place,
public money might not be sufficient to equalize possibilities. This
distinction is important for OC that is sponsored by the government.
Subsidies may help women who would have frozen oocytes otherwise, or
they may increase access for new users.

The need for elective OC and the reasons behind fertility postponement
are the subjects of a second body of research. The most frequently
stated causes of non-medical OC, according to a systematic analysis by
\citet{kynigopoulou2024}, are the lack of a suitable partner,
investments in education and profession, and general economic
uncertainties (2024:4). A common topic is that OC is viewed as a type of
reproductive insurance. Although it offers comfort in the face of
uncertainty, it can' t completely reverse the reduction
in age-related fertility.

Evidence regarding willingness to pay and acceptance helps to further
clarify this image.

\citet{gambadauro2023} conclude that OC is widely accepted as a
technology based on survey data from Swedish university students.
However, when OC is presented as age-related rather than medically
required, support for public funding is significantly lower (2023:6).
There are no significant differences in willingness to pay between
scenarios (2023:10). This implies that debate is more about who should
pay for the technology than it is about its perceived usefulness. Demand
for OC seems to be very variable but price-sensitive from a policy
standpoint in which the main factors are social standards and views of
justice. Both uptake and the distributional effects of public funding
are likely to be influenced by these factors.

From an economic standpoint, the effectiveness and therefore the
efficiency of elective OC depends on two key parameters: age at freezing
and subsequent utilisation. \citet{chronopoulou2021} show that live
birth rates per vitrified oocyte decline sharply with age, and where,
therefore, freezing at younger ages is substantially more effective from
a clinical perspective (2021:112). This age gradient underpins most
economic models of OC cost-effectiveness.

However, clinical potential does not always convert into actual results.
\citet{yang2022} demonstrate, in a retrospective cohort of 2,038 women
followed over seven years, that only 8.4\% returned to use their
vitrified oocytes, yielding a population-level birth rate of just 3.0\%
relative to all women who froze. The cost per live delivery rises
significantly when expenditures are averaged among all freezing women
instead than just those who eventually use OC (2022:5). This distinction
is critical for policy assessment, since when evaluated at the level of
all publicly sponsored recipients, a policy may be significantly less
cost-effective than for the marginal user who freezes and subsequently
utilizes OC. Because utilisation is revealed-preference behaviour that a
price subsidy will only partially shift, the programme cost must be
spread over all recipients, not only those who ultimately attempt a
pregnancy. \citet{loendersloot2011} reach the same conclusion from
their Markov model: OC is cost-effective only under high utilisation
combined with high clinical success, a combination that is empirically
rare.

Inference is made more difficult by the institutional setting. In
addition to notable gaps in standardized data, \citet{shenfield2017}
find a great deal of variation in OC legislation, funding arrangements,
and outcome reporting throughout Europe (2017:152). This reduces the
external validity of cross-country comparisons and stresses the
importance of conducting country-specific evaluations that account for
national institutional circumstances.

The cost-effectiveness of elective OC in comparison to other fertility
methods is formally evaluated in a number of research. Van Loendersloot
et al. (2011) create a Markov model that contrasts a strategy of no
freezing followed by subsequent IVF with elective OC at age 35. They
discover that OC is only cost-effective under stringent assumptions,
necessitating favourable success probabilities and high utilization
rates (2011:3057). Crucially, when assessing cost-effectiveness,
behavioural factors are at least as significant as clinical ones, which
of course has direct implications for policy design.

Based on U.S. SART-CORS data, another recent research by Bakkensen et
al. (2022) contrasts planned OC with later IVF in conjunction with
preimplantation genetic testing. The authors discover that among women
who delay having children, OC at a younger age can lower the anticipated
cost per live delivery and raise the likelihood of reaching the intended
family size (2022:9). However, sensitivity analysis reveal that these
findings are substantially reliant on assumptions about freezing age and
future use (2022:14). Once more, positive outcomes at the individual
level do not always translate to a population-wide, public-financed
solution.

Finally, a tiny but important body of literature takes a public finance
perspective. \citet{matorras2016} estimate the lifetime net fiscal
contribution of people born through assisted reproduction in Spain using
a generational accounting methodology. The discounted value of future
taxes and social contributions surpasses public spending, despite
significant upfront medical expenditures (2016:118). In the base case,
the net fiscal contribution of an IVF-conceived individual is \euro{}66,709
(at 2006 prices), implying a return of \euro{}15.98 per euro of public
investment. This figure is the principal benchmark against which the
programme cost model in Chapter~\ref{chap:economic} is calibrated. Critically, however,
\citet{matorras2016} model clinical IVF for infertile couples; the parameters
governing cost and success for elective OC are materially different: the
upfront vitrification cycle adds a cost category absent from IVF, and
the low utilisation rate means the effective cost per birth is spread
over a much larger denominator. As a result, ART is ultimately
financially lucrative. The approach offers a helpful framework for
expanding evaluation beyond cost per birth to net social returns, even
though it does not particularly address elective OC. By doing this, the
standard is moved from the effectiveness of the health system to the
viewpoint of a social planner who is interested in public finances and
demographic sustainability.

The literature implies that elective OC can be clinically successful and
perhaps cost-efficient when performed at a younger age. However, low
utilization rates, socioeconomic selectivity, and diverse attitudes
toward public funding limit efficiency at the population level.
Furthermore, there is still no causal information about the effect of
elective OC on completed fertility, and a large portion of the current
research is based on calibrated models or observational data. What is
largely unexplored is whether publicly funded elective OC can be
justified once behavioural responses, selection into utilisation and
long-term fiscal effects are considered jointly.

\chapter{Methodology}\label{chap:methodology}
This section provides a detailed description of, and methodological underpinnings for the two analytical pieces of the paper. Our first component (the demographic analysis) captures Spanish fertility dynamics with ITS models, forms counterfactual trajectories, and benchmarks the conditions that a hypothetical OC policy would need to satisfy to yield a quantifiable boost in completed fertility. This aspect is explicitly presented as a calibration exercise rather than a causal evaluation because no real OC programme existed during the estimation window: the ITS identifies structural dynamics and breakpoints in the historical fertility series, not the effects of an implemented policy. The second component (the economic evaluation) utilizes these demographic data to develop a decision-analytic cost model, estimate an ICER relative to the status quo, perform sensitivity analysis, estimate the annual budget impact under three uptake scenarios, and evaluate the distributional consequences of alternative public-funding designs.

\section{Assumptions}\label{assumptions}

OC is best conceptualised as a sequence of clinically established yet
economically heterogeneous stages, as reliance on average treatment
costs risks obscuring substantial variation in marginal resource use.~

The process begins with a gynecologic diagnostic and eligibility assessment to
evaluate ovarian reserve and treatment suitability. This is followed by
controlled ovarian stimulation, during which exogenous gonadotropins are
administered over approximately 10--14 days and ovarian response is
closely monitored through ultrasound examinations and laboratory
testing; this phase typically constitutes the most resource-intensive
component of the intervention. Once adequate follicular maturation is
achieved, oocyte retrieval is performed via ultrasound-guided aspiration
under light anaesthesia. Retrieved oocytes are subsequently processed in
an embryology laboratory and vitrified for long-term storage in liquid
nitrogen \citep{pai2021}.

The official cost for assisted reproduction in the Spanish public health system is not documented. Two published studies estimating the direct healthcare costs of both in-vitro fertilization (IVF) and artificial insemination (AI) cycles in two Spanish institutions formed the basis for the IVF costs \citep{matorrasweinig2011,matorras2001,navarro2006}. The number calculated was cost per live birth, with an average success rate of 28.7 percent \citep{sef2001}. Expected investment for assisted reproduction is \euro{}2661 for IVF and \euro{}1424 for AI (prices for 2006) provided only three cycles of assisted reproductive treatment are funded by the government. Pregnancy will come to a price of \euro{}4173 for IVF and \euro{}3629 with AI \citep{matorras2016}. The model explains that only 75\% of pregnancies will deliver a live baby when estimating costs and benefits. Costs of delivery had not been taken into account by the model, as they are expected to be constant between babies conceived by assisted reproductive treatment and those conceived naturally. Three caveats qualify for direct application of these figures to elective OC. First, all Matorras et al. values are in 2006 prices; Spanish healthcare input costs rose by about 37\% between 2006 and 2023 (INE IPCSNA), so nominal comparisons understate present costs. Secondly, the 28.7\% success rate comes from IVF for clinical infertility and is not directly transferable to elective OC: the age at retrieval, age at thaw, and number of vitrified oocytes interrelate in a non-linear fashion \citep{cobo2016,chronopoulou2021}. Third, elective OC adds a cost element absent from clinical IVF: the vitrification cycle itself (stimulation, retrieval, laboratory processing, multi-year cryostorage), approximated in the range of \euro{}3,000–5,000 at SEF 2023 prices. Cost is incurred regardless of whether the woman ever uses her oocytes, thus making it a fixed programme cost that must be included in any complete budget-impact analysis.

To date, no European country has implemented a large-scale public
funding scheme for elective OC. Existing evidence
is therefore drawn from cross-country variation, eligibility criteria
and partial coverage arrangements.

\section{Estimation via different models}\label{estimation-via-different-models}

According to \citet{stockwatson2020}, when randomised assignment is not
feasible and no reliable control group exists, an ITS design is a
segmented-trend regression framework that can support causal inference
when a genuine policy intervention is externally assigned and the
parallel-trends assumption holds. In the present study, however, no
actual policy intervention took place during the estimation window. The
ITS is therefore applied in a calibration mode: the structural break
identified at $T_0$ = 2010 serves as a hypothetical anchor for the
counterfactual, and the estimated post-break coefficients characterise
the fertility dynamics that a real OC programme implemented at that date
would have needed to replicate in order to generate the observed level
and slope changes. The core concept remains identical: comparing
post-break outcomes to a counterfactual trajectory inferred from the
pre-break trend.

Let $Y_t$ denote an aggregate outcome observed at time $t = 1,\ldots,T$, and let $T_0$ be the date of the policy intervention. Define the binary variable

\begin{equation}
D_t =
\begin{cases}
0 & \text{if } t < T_0,\\
1 & \text{if } t \geq T_0.
\end{cases}
\label{eq:Dt}
\end{equation}
\addequationtolist{Definition of the post-break indicator}

The break indicator $D_t$ in this application is the structural breakpoint detected at $T_0$ = 2010. Note that it doesn't represent an observed policy reform; no publicly funded elective OC programme existed during the estimation window in Spain. The year 2010 was selected as the structural break according to the Euclidean distance criterion described in Section~\ref{intervention-year}, and grounds the counterfactual trajectory relative to which post-break fertility dynamics are assessed. All post-break parameters calculated from this specification should be interpreted as identifying the structural change in fertility at T0, not determining the causal impact of a programme. 

Employing the notation and arrangement utilized by \citet{stockwatson2020} for policy analysis with time trends, our ITS regression model is defined in Equation~\eqref{eq:its-model} and $u_t$ is an unobserved error term.

\begin{equation}
Y_t = \beta_0 + \beta_1 t + \beta_2 D_t + \beta_3 (t - T_0) D_t + u_t
\label{eq:its-model}
\end{equation}
\addequationtolist{Interrupted time series regression model}

\(\beta_{1}\) shows the linear time trend pre-intervention, \(\beta_{2}\) reflects the instantaneous change in the level of the result at the time of intervention and \(\beta_{3}\) indicates the change in the slope of the trend postintervention. As emphasized by \citet{stockwatson2020}, the causal interpretation of \(\beta_{2}\) and \(\beta_{3}\) depends on the assumption that, in the absence of the structural break, the pre-break trend would have continued unchanged. In the context of the present calibration, this assumption is still kept as a benchmark: it determines the counterfactual with which post-2010 fertility is compared, while recognizing that the break in itself reflects multi-faceted simultaneous effects (macroeconomic downturn, cohort effects, and potential for behavioural change) rather than a single identified policy. This population regression model can be formulated in their notation as Equation~\eqref{eq:population-model}, the key exogeneity assumption stated as Equation~\eqref{eq:exogeneity}. Here the regressor vector $X_t$ contains a constant, a linear time trend, the intervention indicator $D_t$, and the interaction term $(t - T_0)D_t$. Under the assumption of exogeneity, OLS yields unbiased and consistent estimators of the coefficient vector $\beta$.

\begin{equation}
Y_t = X_t^{\prime}\beta + u_t
\label{eq:population-model}
\end{equation}
\addequationtolist{Population regression representation of the ITS model}

\begin{equation}
E(u_t \mid X_t) = 0
\label{eq:exogeneity}
\end{equation}
\addequationtolist{Exogeneity condition}

\citet{stockwatson2020} stress that in time series applications the
error term \(u_{t}\) may exhibit serial correlation. While serial
correlation does not bias OLS coefficient estimates, it invalidates
conventional standard errors. Therefore, inference in the ITS
regressions relies on heteroskedasticity- and autocorrelation-consistent
(HAC) standard errors.

In this sense, this aggregate-level analysis provides the macro-level
evidence on the nature of the post-2010 structural change in Spanish
fertility, thereby bounding the demographic conditions under which a
hypothetical OC programme could be fiscally self-sustaining.
Nevertheless, while the ITS analysis focuses on aggregate outcomes, the
underlying behavioural mechanisms operate at the individual level. To
complement the aggregate analysis, this research follows the discrete
choice framework presented in the aforementioned. Let $Y_i$ be a
binary variable equal to 1 if individual $i$ delays motherhood beyond a
given age threshold and 0 otherwise. \citet{stockwatson2020} motivate the logit
model using the latent-variable formulation in Equations~\eqref{eq:latent-rule} and~\eqref{eq:latent-index}.

\begin{equation}
Y_i =
\begin{cases}
1 & \text{if } Y_i^{*} > 0,\\
0 & \text{if } Y_i^{*} \leq 0.
\end{cases}
\label{eq:latent-rule}
\end{equation}
\addequationtolist{Latent binary response rule}

\begin{equation}
Y_i^{*} = X_i^{\prime}\beta + u_i
\label{eq:latent-index}
\end{equation}
\addequationtolist{Latent index equation}

Assuming that $u_i$ follows a logistic distribution leads to the
logit specification shown in Equation~\eqref{eq:logit-prob},

\begin{equation}
P(Y_i = 1 \mid X_i) = \frac{e^{X_i^{\prime}\beta}}{1 + e^{X_i^{\prime}\beta}}
\label{eq:logit-prob}
\end{equation}
\addequationtolist{Logit probability specification}

The parameters \(\beta\) are estimated by maximum likelihood;
coefficients indicate the direction of the effect of each covariate;
while marginal effects are used to assess economic
significance\footnote{An important limitation of this specification is
  that the dependent variable (is\_adjTFR\_low\_fertility) is
  constructed endogenously from the same adjTFR series used in the
  linear ITS, creating a risk of circular identification: the logit may
  recover the structural break already detected by the linear model
  rather than providing independent information. The near-perfect AUC (=
  1.00) documented in Appendix~\ref{app:model:roc} is consistent with quasi-complete
  separation driven by this circularity. The logit results should
  therefore be interpreted as a qualitative robustness check on the
  direction of the ITS finding, not as an independent source of
  identification.}.

\section{Intervention year}\label{intervention-year}

In any ITS design, the choice of the intervention year is a crucial one in ITS identification, which controls the temporal partition to provide pre-intervention trends and post-intervention dynamic responses. For this study, the intervention year is $T_0$ = 2010. To derive the decision of $T_0$ from a formal metric, we compute the Euclidean distance of the multivariate fertility profile of Spain. The 2020 observation is treated as the terminal observation and is compared with each previous year in the dataset. This distance-minimisation criterion provides a benchmark for identifying the year in which Spain's fertility structure most closely resembled its 2020 configuration, thereby making the beginning of the structural regime that characterises the post-break period. An important limitation of this approach needs to be recognised: 2020 is the terminal observation of the analysis of that series to determine the pre-trend, which is that it is vulnerable to look-ahead bias; it chooses the breakpoint by reference to the very endpoint the ITS tries to model. Figure~\ref{fig:euclidean} shows that Spain (2019) minimises the Euclidean distance to Spain (2020) with distances increasing monotonically as you proceed further back in time. The distance for Spain (2010) marks the outer boundary of this neighbourhood, beyond which the fertility profile differs markedly from the post-break configuration. This pattern is consistent with interpreting 2010 as the structural breakpoint separating a pre-crisis fertility regime from a relatively low-fertility post-crisis steady state. 

From a design perspective, $T_0$ = 2010 provides a pre-break window of 34 observations (1976--2009) and a post-break window of 13 observations (2010--2022). While this partition is not symmetric, the pre-break sample is sufficiently long to identify the baseline trend reliably, and the post-break window is adequate to estimate slope corrections. The chosen breakpoint exhibits internal consistency within all empirical specifications: the linear ITS framework recovers a large and statistically significant level shift alongside variation in post-break slope, while the logit specification highlights a sharp shift to a low-fertility regime around 2010, followed by a gradual moderation in the post-break fertility trajectory. It bears emphasis that this internal consistency is a property of the statistical fit of the chosen $T_0$, not evidence that 2010 marks the introduction of a policy; both specifications recover the same historical break in fertility dynamics, which reflects the compound impact of demographic, economic and cohort-level forces coming together around that date. A key design feature of this study should be stated explicitly here and carried through all subsequent sections. No real publicly funded elective OC programme existed in Spain during the estimation period. Accordingly, the ITS exercise in this study is a simulated breakpoint design in which the structural break at $T_0$ is a hypothetical anchor for the counterfactual, not a quasi-experimental assessment of a real policy change. All references to post-break effects, post-break dynamics, and post-2010 trajectories in the rest of this paper must be understood strictly in that sense: they explain the fertility dynamics linked to the historically recognized structural break, not the causal impact of an established OC programme. This framing governs every interpretative claim in Sections~\ref{chap:demographic}, \ref{chap:economic}, and \ref{chap:discussion}. 

