\chapter{Demographic evaluation}\label{chap:demographic}
\section{Post-2010 Structural Break Dynamics}\label{post-2010-structural-break-dynamics}

The ITS results described here, in the first part, describe the fertility dynamics associated with $T_0$ = 2010. This breakpoint is defined in Section~\ref{intervention-year} and does not represent an actual policy implementation; and the estimated coefficients do not identify the causal effects of publicly funded OC. Instead, it captures the deviation of observed fertility from the pre-2010 counterfactual trajectory; a deviation that is then used to come up with the conditions for a hypothetical OC programme to satisfy, in order to generate demographic additionality. The fundamental diagnostic question is whether the post-break dynamics feature the age-specific recovery that a properly performing OC programme predicted: partial recuperation of fertility at 35–39 years (oocytes used after a delay) and continued fertility at ages 40–44 (as seen with successful use in the target age window). In principle, a policy that facilitates postponement might affect fertility through timing adjustments, but whether these translate into higher completed fertility depends on whether later-life biological constraints outweigh the gains from postponement.

So the empirical question is: does this post-2010 structural break demonstrate the age-specific recuperation at ages 35-44 that a significant OC programme would need to generate? The answer immediately conditions the economic assessment in Chapter~\ref{chap:economic}: if partial recovery is observed at 35–39 but not at 40–44, the demographic additionality parameter α must be calibrated to its lower range that mechanically increases the estimated cost per additional birth and dampens the programme’s fiscal return.


