\section{Age-Specific Fertility Rates results}\label{age-specific-fertility-rates-results}

To begin with, the post-break difference between fitted and counterfactual age specific fertility rates (ASFRs) for women aged 35–39 is negative in the years immediately following 2010. This indicates that the observed fertility rate for this age group falls below the level predicted by continuation of the pre-break trend. Over time, however, the magnitude of this negative gap progressively declines. In later post-break years, the difference approaches zero and becomes slightly positive, indicating that observed fertility is converging toward, and will eventually marginally exceed, the counterfactual trajectory, a pattern consistent with partial delayed-childbearing recuperation at this age group. 

For women aged 40–44, however, the pattern looks different. For this age group, the post-break gap between fitted and counterfactual ASFRs remains predominantly negative throughout the post-2010 period. Fertility at ages 40–44 consistently lies below the level predicted by continuation of the pre-break trend. There is, as opposed to the 35–39 age group, no sustained convergence toward the counterfactual trajectory over time; direct evidence with implications for the demographic additionality parameter, in Section~\ref{cost-per-additional-birth}.

These differences are also expressed in the linear ITS estimates and the predicted probabilities derived from the logit specification. The 35–39 age group shows a narrowing gap after the structural break, while the 40–44 age group consistently deviates negatively from its counterfactual trajectory throughout the post-2010 period. 

This asymmetry has a specific relevance for the economic evaluation. If an OC programme targets women aged 30–35, their oocytes would typically be used at ages 38–45, exactly the age window where the ASFR remains below its counterfactual. This suggests that even women who successfully use their oocytes would be operating within conditions of structural fertility suppression, reducing the likelihood that the resulting birth is indeed additional. The demographic additionality parameter, alpha, should thus be calibrated toward its lower range (0.3-0.5), which mechanically raises the estimated cost per additional birth.


\chapter{Economic evaluation}\label{chap:economic}
\section{Building on Matorras' discussion}\label{building-on-matorras-discussion}

Infertility affects 15\% of Spain’s population, or approximately one in seven couples of reproductive age \citep{matorrasweinig2011}. Today, the Spanish public health care system provides two approaches to assisted reproduction: IVF and AI. Public financing only covers a maximum of three IVF cycles or four AI cycles, or some combination of both, irrespective of whether pregnancy is achieved. Eligibility is limited to females between the ages of 18 and 40 years who are physically and mentally fit, categorized as infertile, and without any evidence of decreased ovarian reserve, with priority given to nulliparous infertile couples \citep{alberto2002}. Despite this framework, demand outstrips supply: in 2010, about one million couples requested treatment, but just 22 percent received at least one cycle, 65\% of which was done in private institutions. Average waiting times in the public system hit 339 days, creating both extra demand and inequity in access. This perceived access gap is the appropriate beginning of an equity argument in favour of publicly funded elective OC: if clinical IVF is already publicly funded for medical indications, rejecting elective OC as an elective due to its cost while tolerating a 14-fold socioeconomic gradient in private access \citep{alonpinilla2021} presents a tension with the Spanish National Health System (SNS) principle of universal coverage. Yet, mere equity in access does not justify public expenditure without presenting evidence that the programme accomplishes meaningful health or demographic outcomes at a reasonable cost. The generational accounting framework and the economic evaluation in Section~\ref{chap:economic} present the two sides of this assessment. 

From a macro-fiscal perspective, these access constraints compound with wider demographic pressures. Previous research suggests that even under optimistic estimates, a shrinking birth rate and population ageing can produce fiscal imbalances that can only be mitigated by cuts in government spending or increases in taxation \citep{imw2012,mess2006,nrc2012,perezcamarero2012} Higher fertility would also contribute to a medium-term growth of the taxpaying population, partially offsetting the fiscal pressures of ageing. In this context, public support for ART could alleviate this inequity by improving access and promoting fiscal stability in the long run. Generational accounting models estimate the social net fiscal contribution (NFC) of a person born through ART. The model calculates the present value of all gross revenues received by the State from the individual over the life cycle, minus direct transfer expenditures. Transfers and taxes are apportioned based on the risk of an individual’s falling into various socioeconomic classes (employment, education, unemployment, business ownership) in each year of life. The base year corresponds to the imaginary year of birth, and baseline macroeconomic measures, especially GDP growth and unemployment, are assumed constant over the projection timeline. 

As discussed in Section~\ref{assumptions}, cost inputs for Spain come from published estimates of direct healthcare costs for IVF and AI-cycle applications \citep{matorrasweinig2011,matorras2001,navarro2006}. Based on an average success rate of 28.7\% \citep{sef2001}, the expected investment per live birth is assumed to be \euro{}4,173 for IVF and \euro{}3,629 for AI (2006 prices), reflecting that 75\% of pregnancies end in a live birth. Delivery costs are excluded, since they are presumed similar between assisted and natural conceptions. All cost data quoted from \citet{matorras2001} are denominated in 2006 euros. In order to ease comparison with the 2023 price cost estimates, a nominal upward adjustment of approximately 37\% is needed, assessed using the INE IPCSNA deflator for 2006–2023. In prices as at 2023, the expected IVF investment per live birth is approximately \euro{}5,717, and the NFC for an IVF-conceived individual is around \euro{}91,391.

Generational accounting findings indicate that while children are net beneficiaries of public transfers at an early stage, the cumulative fiscal balance turns positive at working age and stays so until retirement. For the base case, the lifetime's present value of taxes paid by a naturally conceived individual is \euro{}370,482, which is much larger than the transfers amounting to \euro{}275,972, resulting in an NFC of \euro{}94,510. After treatment costs, the NFC of an IVF-conceived individual is \euro{}66,709 and \euro{}67,253 for AI, implying returns of \euro{}15.98 and \euro{}18.53 per euro invested, respectively. The break-even age falls between 29 and 41 years, depending on macroeconomic assumptions. 

Sensitivity analysis indicates that NFC is still positive during many macroeconomic scenarios, but can have negative returns in extreme adverse scenarios. International evidence similarly shows that ART-conceived individuals have positive lifetime fiscal contributions in most countries, with the notable exception of the Netherlands \citep{moolenaar2014}. But those are context-dependent results and they vary by national fiscal structures. 

Important caveats apply. Around 20\% of IVF pregnancies result in multiple births \citep{bentov2025}, and the likelihood of higher returns when more than one child is born per funded cycle may be increased. But multiple pregnancies also carry higher risks of prematurity and neonatal complications, which may include other healthcare costs. If funding increases, complementary resources might be needed to handle these externalities. In conclusion, previous generational accounting findings demonstrate that public funds provided to ART in Spain should likely achieve positive net fiscal benefits across the life cycle of ART-conceived persons at the same time that it increases equity of access. Yet, these projections are affected by macro stability, utilization rates and demographic response. Therefore, long-run fiscal sustainability largely relies not just on the per-birth fiscal return but also whether publicly funded interventions produce additional births rather than merely shifting their timing. 

Note that the important analytic step not undertaken by \citet{matorras2001} is to embed the NFC estimate within a complete cost-effectiveness model that incorporates (i) the vitrification cost layer specific to elective OC; (ii) the empirically observed utilisation rate, which determines how many women must freeze oocytes per live birth; (iii) the age-stratified success rate at thaw, which is substantially below the clinical-IVF average used in \citet{matorras2001}; and (iv) the demographic additionality factor, which confines OC-assisted births to some portion of births by capping the proportion of OC-assisted births that in reality represent a net increase in completed fertility. Once these four adjustments are applied, the effective fiscal return per euro of public OC expenditure falls considerably below \euro{}15.98 reported for clinical IVF and under most empirically plausible parameter combinations below the fiscal break-even point.



\section{Parameters}\label{parameters}

Drawing on the generational accounting framework outlined in \citet{matorras2016} for IVF, an NFC of \euro{}66,709 is projected per IVF-conceived individual, equating to \euro{}15.98 per euro invested at 2006 pricing. An extension of this framework to elective OC requires that the cost numerator and the effectiveness denominator be both adjusted. The following discussion builds a cost-per-additional-birth framework for publicly funded elective OC that explicitly includes the two metrics most important for programme efficiency: the oocyte utilisation rate and the age-stratified success rate per thaw cycle.

\subsection{Utilisation rates}\label{utilisation-rates}

The percentage of women that use their vitrified oocytes is the sole major factor of the overall cost-effectiveness of the programme. The recorded utilisation rates in the published cohort studies are much lower than that perceived by programme designers. In the retrospective cohort of 2,038 women undergoing elective OC in Taiwan spanning seven years, \citet{yang2022} found that only 8.4\% of participants returned to employ their oocytes, presenting a live birth rate of 3.0\% relative to all women who froze. In Spain, \citet{cobo2018}, in a Spanish private clinic sample of 1,241 women, reported a 12.1\% return rate over a median follow-up of 4.2 years. Rates are as low as 5\% in UK private settings \citet{trokoudes2011}. Findings are in agreement with qualitative evidence in \citet{kynigopoulou2024}, which showed that elective OC acts largely as reproductive insurance; a purchase that reduces anxiety for future infertility, but rarely practiced. Such low utilisation is not a market failure that can be fixed (e.g., via public subsidy) but indicates the preferences of users who value the option, not the outcome.

\subsection{Age-stratified success rates}\label{age-stratified-success-rates}

An analysis in the body of this paper is based on a mean success rate of 28.7\% \citep{sef2001} for IVF cycles done for clinical infertility. For two reasons, this number does not relate to elective OC directly. First, elective OC is conducted most often between ages 30 and 37, with oocytes typically not used until ages 38–44, where biological age upon retrieval and age at utilisation co-determine success. Second, the number of mature oocytes vitrified per cycle is a key continuous moderator of success: in a study appeared in \textit{Fertility and Sterility}, \citet{cobo2016} estimate that a woman who vitrifies five oocytes has a cumulative live birth probability of about 22\% (at age $<35$), relative to 72\% if fifteen or more oocytes have been obtained. Additional evidence of cumulative live birth rates by age at freezing and number of vitrified oocytes, calibrated on the data of Cobo et al. (2016) and Chronopoulou et al. (2021), reinforces this point. The figure highlights that the frequently reported average success rate substantially overestimates results for women who freeze less than ten oocytes or who get them after 37 years of age, the mode of profile for elective users.

\subsection{Cost per additional birth}\label{cost-per-additional-birth}

Let $C_u$ denote the cost of a vitrification cycle including five-year storage (\euro{}5,000 at SEF 2023 prices), $C_T$ the cost of a thaw-and-transfer IVF cycle (\euro{}2,500), $u$ the utilisation rate, $s$ the success rate per thaw cycle, and $\alpha$ the demographic additionality factor (the share of OC-assisted births that would not have occurred by any other means). The expected cost per additional live birth is

\begin{equation}
\text{Cost per birth} = \frac{C_u + u C_T}{u s \alpha}.
\end{equation}
\addequationtolist{Expected cost per additional live birth}

At the base-case parameters ($u = 0.08$, $s = 0.30$, $\alpha = 0.50$), the cost per additional birth is \euro{}433,333.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.88\textwidth]{media/media/image9.png}
\caption{Cost per additional birth and sensitivity to utilisation and success rate}
\label{fig:cost-sensitivity}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:cost-sensitivity} shows a bidimensional sensitivity analysis over the plausible ranges of $u$ and $s$ with $\alpha$ kept constant at 0.50. Costs range from \euro{}137,500 (optimistic: $u = 0.20$, $s = 0.40$) to above \euro{}1{,}000{,}000 (pessimistic: $u = 0.05$, $s = 0.20$). At no point in the plausible parameter space does the cost per birth fall below the \euro{}91,391 net fiscal contribution per additional birth. In empirical utilisation ranges (5--12\%), the cost per additional birth lies between \euro{}433{,}333 and \euro{}683{,}333, far exceeding any defensible willingness-to-pay threshold for a publicly funded pro-natalist intervention.

The tornado chart in Figure~\ref{fig:tornado} illustrates how the utilisation rate is by far the primary uncertainty driver: varying $u$ from 5\% to 20\% alone shifts the cost per birth by more than \euro{}500{,}000, dwarfing the sensitivity to any other parameter.

The comparison of cost per additional birth to NFC adjusted for inflation from \citet{matorras2016} illustrates the financial arithmetic of the programme. Two price bases should be used throughout this calculation. All projected costs of this study were calculated in 2023 prices. The \citet{matorras2016} base-case NFC of \euro{}66,709 is denominated in 2006 prices; applying the INE IPCSNA healthcare cost deflator of 37\% (2006–2023) yields an adjusted NFC of about \euro{}91,391 at 2023 prices. At the base-case parameters ($u = 0.08$, $s = 0.30$, $\alpha = 0.50$), the cost per additional live birth is estimated to be \euro{}433,333 and the gross fiscal return per euro of public expenditure is an estimated \euro{}0.21 (\euro{}91,391 / \euro{}433,333); well below break-even and dramatically less than the \euro{}15.98 return predicted for clinical IVF. According to the pessimistic utilisation scenario ($u = 0.05$, $s = 0.20$), costs per birth exceed \euro{}1{,}025{,}000 and the fiscal return falls to \euro{}0.09 per euro invested, implying a structural fiscal loss of more than \euro{}0.91 per euro spent. The programme is thus not self-financing under any empirically plausible parameter combination. \FloatBarrier

The fiscal self-sustainability condition requires:

\begin{equation}
u s \alpha \geq \frac{C_u + u C_T}{NFC}
\label{eq:fiscal-condition}
\end{equation}
\addequationtolist{Fiscal self-sustainability condition}

Because $u$ appears on both sides, this condition is nonlinear and must be evaluated numerically. At the inflation-adjusted NFC of \euro{}91,391, holding $\alpha = 0.60$ and $s = 0.35$ at their upper plausible bounds, the break-even utilisation rate is $u \approx 0.30$, approximately 3.8 times the empirical average. \FloatBarrier
Under the more conservative 2006 NFC of \euro{}66,709, the required utilisation rate rises to $u \approx 0.43$, more than five times the observed average. The programme thus fails the fiscal self-sustainability condition on either price basis by a large margin at any empirically plausible combined parameter values.

\section{Budget impact}\label{budget-impact}

It is estimated that Spain has about 1.8 million women aged 30-35 \citep{ine2023}.

