Figure~\ref{fig:budget-scenarios} illustrates three uptake scenarios and the annual budget commitment for each. With a 5\% uptake, nearly 90,000 women would freeze annually. At the stated vitrification cost of \euro{}5,000 per cycle, the gross vitrification expenditure alone is \euro{}450M per year. Adding expected thaw-and-transfer IVF costs (approximately \euro{}18M, based on 8\% utilisation and \euro{}2,500 per cycle) and administration (\euro{}2M), annual programme costs total approximately \euro{}470M. Under a 10\% uptake scenario, annual costs reach approximately \euro{}938M; at 20\% uptake, they exceed \euro{}1.87Bn, equivalent to nearly 2.3\% of total Spanish public health expenditure \citep{minsan2024}. The costs of treating adverse events (see Section~\ref{adverse-event-costs}) are not included within these estimates and would add a further 5--8\% to direct procedure costs.

The scale of these budgetary commitments warrants explicit note. Spain is currently spending around \euro{}1.9Bn each year on family and child policy \citep{oecd2024}. Comparative demographic evidence indicates that affordable childcare, wage-replacement parental leave, and housing support interventions tend to achieve stronger and more consistent effects on completed fertility compared to ART-based programmes \citep{gauthier2007,rindfuss2010}. Even the minimum (5\% uptake) scenario would consume approximately \euro{}470M per year, equivalent to roughly 25\% of Spain's entire annual family and child policy budget, for a programme whose cost per additional birth exceeds the per-birth net fiscal contribution under all empirically plausible parameter values. This opportunity cost should be a key factor in any cost-effective consideration of the measure proposed by the Popular Party.

\section{Distributive analysis}\label{distributive-analysis}

The distributive implications of public funding for elective OC depend on two dynamic factors: the economic status of existing private users and the form of the public program.

