\caption{Estimated OC utilisation rates by income quintile under alternative funding designs}
\label{fig:quintiles}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:quintiles} shows how OC utilisation rates are estimated by income quintile in the current private market and in two other designs for public funding, which was calibrated based on the access gradients that \citet{alonpinilla2021} and \citet{seiz2023} noted. The statistics expose a sharply pro-rich profile in the current private access: the estimated utilisation in the top quintile is approximately fourteen times that of the bottom quintile. A universal public subsidy reduces this gradient but does not abolish an existing gradient either, given that informational barriers to specialist centres, geographic access, and cultural attitudes towards ART have been consistently and continuously associated with socioeconomic status. A means-tested subsidy allocated to women of income quintiles Q1–Q3 provides a near-neutral distribution of programme gains, while substantially reducing the fiscal burden.

Figure~\ref{fig:concentration} shows stylised concentration curves \citep{kakwani1977} corresponding to each of the three designs, where the curve located nearer to the line of equality indicates that the distribution of benefits was far more neutral for the two designs. The private markets as they exist currently yield a concentration index of approximately 0.42, strongly pro-rich, calibrated on the actual Spanish income quintile shares (World Bank WDI 2023) and the empirically documented Q5/Q1 access gradient of 14 \citep{alonpinilla2021}. For universal public funding, this is approximately reduced to 0.18, where it approaches distributional neutrality for means-tested funding (the concentration index ($\approx 0.05$)). 
This interpretation has direct consequences for policy design if the declared goal of public OC funding is to improve equity of access to reproductive technology, then a means-tested instrument is clearly preferable to a universal subsidy on both efficiency and equity grounds.

Three alternative institutional designs should be assessed against the income targeting dimension. First, if public delivery of ART thorugh existing ART units, rather than through a demand-side subsidy to private clinics, were feasible, this would integrate the programme with the already existing governance and quality control regime in the SNS, preventing the principal-agent problems outlined in Section~\ref{provider-incentives}. At present, however, the Spanish public ART system has average waiting times of 339 days, so there conditions are not met (\textit{vid. supra}); it would likely prolong waiting times for clinically indicated IVF to have its eligibility expanded to elective OC without a matching expansion of capacity. Second, a progressive co-payment system (similar to a pharmaceutical co-payment system in Spain) may support partial recovery of costs for higher income users, while reserving access for lower income users. Third, a mandatory counselling session on age-specific chances of success and realistic likelihood to use vitrified oocytes should be included in any design, an outcome that prospective users consistently overestimate in the literature \citep{kynigopoulou2024,gambadauro2023}.

\section{Adverse event costs}\label{adverse-event-costs}

Costs associated with adverse events should be included in any comprehensive cost estimate, although they are omitted from the base-case analysis. Ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome (OHSS) is the major risk for controlled ovarian stimulation: moderate OHSS is present in 3-6\% of affected stimulation cycles and severe OHSS requiring hospitalisation in 0.5-2\% \citep{luke2012, pai2021}. In the base scenario of 180,000 cycles per year (i.e. 10\% uptake), severe OHSS would generate approximately 900-3,600 hospitalisations per year, at an average cost of \euro{}3,500-\euro{}8,000 per episode, based on \citet{minsan2024} tariffs. This implies an annual adverse event cost of \euro{}3M-\euro{}29M above and beyond direct procedure costs. Follicular puncture complications (haemorrhage, infection, anaesthetic risk) are less common but not negligible at population scale, with ~0.3\% of retrievals. A publicly funded programme should incorporate these externalities into its budget envelope from the outset.

A further long-run cost category should be acknowledged.

The long-run safety profile of children born from vitrified oocytes has not yet been fully quantified; because vitrification protocols were standardised only between 2006 and 2008 and long-term follow-up data are restricted to about 15 years. Present evidence does not show higher risk of congenital anomalies, or adverse developmental outcomes compared to conventionally conceived children or offspring of original IVF \citep{cobo2022,noyes2009}. However, the existing evidente is not sufficiently powered to detect rare outcomes at the frequency that would become epidemiologically visible if a large public programme dramatically expanded the cohort of vitrification-conceived individuals. A publicly funded programme of the scale outlined here would essentially be a population-level experiment whose long-term safety data would not be available for two decades. That irreversibility needs to be taken into consideration any precautionary policy analysis.

\section{Provider incentives}\label{provider-incentives}

The Spanish ART market is predominantly private. In 2021, approximately 78\% of all the ART cycles were undertaken in private institutions \citep{sef2024registro}. Private ART providers face a structural financial incentive to expand the uptake of elective OC, as it is a high-margin, recurring-revenue service, with relatively low marginal cost per additional patient in facilities that are already staffed to provide ovarian stimulation. The introduction of a public subsidy would expand the pool of solvent demand by reducing price barriers. This would bring in women who are currently deterred by cost, thereby directly increasing private-clinic revenues without needing to change ownership or regulation structure of provision.

This triggers an example of supplier-induced demand risk \citep{evans1974}. \citet{deonandan2012} document that ART providers systematically exaggerate the odds of successful treatment in counselling interactions, and that the marketing of elective OC tends to conflate the probability of having frozen oocytes with a probability of giving live birth, a distinction that is highly consequential in practice.

A demand-side subsidy channelled through private firms without price regulation, compulsory outcome disclosure or private counselling obligations would subject the public payer to moral hazard from both the supply side (demand created by providers) and the demand side (women who freeze but would never have used the oocytes, a situation that creates pure deadweight cost). The regulatory conditions to minimize these risks (tariff regulation, accreditation guidelines, compulsory pre-treatment counselling by an independent clinician, public reporting on clinic-level outcomes disaggregated by age and number of oocytes ) are lacking from the present Spanish regulatory environment as well as the PP policy proposal in press \citep{partidopopular2025}. Their absence is not a minor implementation detail; rather, a structural flaw that would drive programme costs high without improving outcomes.

\section{Incremental Cost-Effectiveness Ratio}\label{icer}

This analysis in this section can be summed up by one ICER; this calculation is found in relation to the comparator of no public subsidy (status quo whereby elective OC is privately financed). The incremental cost is the net public expenditure per eligible woman under the proposed design. The incremental effectiveness is defined as the expected additional live births attributable to the programme, controlling for demographic additionality. The base-case ICER for publicly funded elective OC relative to no intervention is \euro{}433,333 per additional live birth, with a sensitivity range of \euro{}137,500 (optimistic) to above \euro{}1{,}000{,}000 (pessimistic).

As for a clear willingness-to-pay threshold for live births as a policy outcome, there is none within the Spanish health system, and the NHS framework is based upon quality-adjusted life years (QALYs), with an implicit threshold estimate being about \euro{}25,000--30,000/QALY \citep{vallejotorres2018}. Applying QALY-value terminology to the ICER requires assumptions on the quality-of-life benefit to society and to parents for an additional child, a contentious and methodologically challenging valuation, but under the assumption of 20 full-quality life years per expected life, a \euro{}433,333 cost per birth is equivalent to \euro{}21,667/QALY, which lies within the conventional threshold. The calculation, however, is dominated by the contested NFC projection and by the assumption of 20 full-quality life years per birth, assumptions that are highly uncertain and sensitive to long-run macroeconomic conditions. The fiscal self-sustainability condition computed above requires a utilisation rate approximately 3.8 times the empirical average under optimistic clinical assumptions, and this condition is effectively unattainable under current realised parameters. Even where the QALY cost falls within the threshold range, publicly funded elective OC cannot be deemed justified on fiscal self-sustainability grounds at any empirically meaningful parameter value.

\chapter{Discussion}\label{chap:discussion}
This paper aimed to assess whether publicly funded elective OC could raise medium-term fertility, satisfy a fiscal sustainability condition, and represent a cost-effective use of public expenditure relative to alternative policy instruments. The analysis adresses these three goals through two analytical components of an ITS design based on Spanish demographic data for 1976–2022 that describes the structural fertility dynamics relevant to the breakpoint identified in 2010, and bounds the degree of demographic additionality required for programme feasibility, and a complete economic assessment in the form of a decision-analytic cost model, ICER computation, sensitivity analysis, budget impact projections, and distributional analysis. Results yield little evidence that the programme fulfills all three criteria together, but the equity argument is nonetheless relevant under a means-tested design.

Age-specific effects show diverse timing adjustments with no evidence of complete compensation. Women aged 35--39 years exhibited a post-break gap in ASFRs relative to the counterfactual, which gradually narrowed then became slightly positive, indicating partial recovery as expected in delayed childbearing\footnote{At the aggregate level, the structural break for $T_0$ = 2010 yields a significant level shift in adjTFR, with no convergence toward the pre-break counterfactual. Although the coefficient on post-break slope partially cancels out the existing downward trajectory, the post-break deviation from the counterfactual is estimated to remain negative over all post-2010 period. Fertility outcomes are persistently below their predicted trajectory, indicating not a short-run fluctuation, but a structural change in the fertility regime.}. By contrast, after 2010, fertility at ages 40–44 remains permanently below its counterfactual trajectory. The absence of recovery at the oldest age intervals of reproduction implies that the postponement is only partially offset and that some fertility losses are not recovered, a pattern inconsistent with complete demographic additionality from an OC programme targeting the 30–35 demographic age range.

The linear ITS and logit specifications produce consistent results. In addition, the linear ITS model demonstrates significant level and slope changes of fertility outcomes at the structural break while the logit specification\footnote{Which should be considered as a qualitative robustness check on the same structural break and not as a sole factor of identification, since the dependent variable is derived from the same adjTFR series.} indicates a lower probability of observing high-fertility outcomes after 2010. The stability of coefficients across specifications and their robustness to alternative assumptions support the interpretation of the post-2010 dynamics as structural rather than transitory or cyclic. In total, the evidence suggests that the structural conditions prevailing after the 2010 break are associated with a sustained net decline in aggregate fertility relative to the pre-break counterfactual, mitigated by timing at ages 35–39, but not compensated at 40–44. This means that a hypothetical OC programme at $T_0$ = 2010 would have been operating within a demographic context that is unfavourable to the generation of substantial fertility additionality under empirically plausible programme conditions.

Within the generational accounting framework of \citet{matorras2016} the fiscal return on publicly financed assisted reproduction relies upon the generation of more persons who make a positive impact over the life course. The current results imply that public policies, focused predominantly on postponement, may not lead to the demographic additionality necessary for such fiscal returns to be realized at population level. Thus, while per-birth net fiscal contributions per capita may still be positive, macro-fiscal sustainability of publicly funded elective OC seems to be limited in the absence of a demonstrable increase in completed fertility. The economic assessment adds to and qualifies these findings in three significant respects. First, taking aside the demographic evidence, the cost-per-additional-birth analysis indicates that publicly funded elective OC is unlikely to become a self-sustainable financial process given empirically plausible utilisation and success rates. In the base case ($u = 0.08$, $s = 0.30$, $\alpha = 0.50$), the cost per extra live birth is ca.\ \euro{}433,333, compared with the inflation-adjusted net fiscal contribution of an OC-conceived individual (\euro{}91,391 at 2023 prices, updated from the \citet{matorras2016} base-case of \euro{}66,709 at 2006 prices using the INE IPCSNA deflator). The gross fiscal return per euro spent is therefore approximately \euro{}0.21, well below break-even and dramatically below the \euro{}15.98 return estimated for clinical IVF by \citet{matorras2016}. Under pessimistic but empirically verifiable utilisation rates ($u = 0.05$, $s = 0.20$), costs per birth exceed \euro{}1{,}025{,}000 and the fiscal return falls to approximately \euro{}0.09 per euro, implying a structural fiscal loss of more than \euro{}0.91 per euro of public expenditure. The break-even utilisation rate, holding $s = 0.35$ and $\alpha = 0.60$ at their upper plausible bounds, is approximately 0.30, which is roughly 3.8 times the empirical average. Second, the cost of a national programme would run to approximately \euro{}470M (5\% uptake) to over \euro{}1.87Bn (20\% uptake) per year, and the additional costs of adverse events would not be factored into the direct procedure budget. Third, the distributional analysis demonstrates that a universal subsidy is mildly pro-wealthy in some respects; furthermore, a means-tested design aimed at income quintiles Q1-Q3 would experience near distributional neutrality at a lower fiscal cost; essentially putting it ahead over a universal subsidy in terms of fairness and efficiency if the goal of redistributing access is explicitly mentioned.

Three policy implications emerge: First, the benefits of publicly funded elective OC should not be justified on either pro-natalist or fiscal sustainability grounds; the ITS evidence and cost model together suggest that such justification would entail implausibly optimistic assumptions of both demographic impact and utilisation. If the policy moves forward it should be articulated according to autonomy and equity principles (lowering financial obstacles to a reproductive option lower-income women currently cannot access) with full transparency regarding the low probability of future use of the procedure and the only moderate increase of live birth probability it engenders. Second, any programme should include compulsory pre-treatment counselling by an independent clinician and public reporting of age- and oocyte-stratified success rates, and tariff regulation to discourage supplier induced demand in the largely private ART market. Third, the \euro{}470M--1.87Bn annual cost of the OC subsidy programme could be compared with evidence-based pro-natalist policies (such as affordable childcare and the replacement of parental leave wage labour) with better-documented demographic effectiveness and distributionally progressive effects.

