\chapter{Annex I. Model}\label{app:model}
\section[Dataset description and preprocessing]{Dataset description and preprocessing\protect\footnotemark}
\label{dataset-description-and-preprocessing}
\footnotetext{Replication materials for this study, including the code and data underlying the empirical analysis, are available at \url{https://github.com/alejandrocorchonfranco/reproeconomics}.}

The empirical analysis is conducted on yearly demographic and macroeconomic data for Spain, derived from official sources (INE, World Bank, Human Fertility Database) and integrated into a single longitudinal data set covering 1976 to 2022. The estimation uses 47 annual observations, providing a long pre-break period and a sufficient post-break horizon for the ITS analysis. For the macroeconomic series, World Bank data extends to 2022 and was used without extrapolation. One possible issue with an annual macroeconomic time series of this length is non-stationarity, because integrating fertility and macroeconomic series of order one (I(1)) without cointegration testing risks generating spurious regressions \citep{grangernewbold1974}. Visual checks suggest that the adjTFR and Female LFP series are borderline I(1) and GDP growth borderline I(0). The ITS specification alleviates some of these issues by incorporating a deterministic linear trend, through which the effect of I(1) can be absorbed when the series is trend-stationary.

The key demographic variables include general fertility rates and age specific fertility behaviour. The data set covers adjTFR and parity metrics such as first-birth fertility (adjTFR1) and third- or higher-order fertility (adjTFR3plus). To specifically capture the delayed motherhood, we also include ASFRs for women aged 35--39 (ASFR\_(35--39)) and 40--44 (ASFR\_(40--44)) in the dataset. Exposure variables, denoting the population at risk in each age group (Exposure\_(15\_49), Exposure\_(35\_39), Exposure\_(40\_44)), are incorporated to normalize demographic composition.

The data have linear time trend (time), binary break indicator, (intervention) and interaction (post\_intervention\_trend) for the identification of structural breaks. It is worth noting that the variable label `intervention' in the dataset specifies the structural breakpoint indicator at $T_0$ = 2010, and it does not indicate an intervention that is observed. Furthermore, a binary indicator for low-fertility regimes (is\_adjTFR\_low\_fertility) is added for the complementary logit robustness analysis. Macroeconomic covariates are added to the data set to account for general economic variables that might affect fertility decisions. These are related to the rate of real economy growth (GDP\_Growth\_PC), participation of females in the labour force (Female\_LFP), and the rate of youth unemployment (Juvenile\_Unemployment\_Rate). Due to data constraints, the latter two variables are observed for shorter subperiods and included in sensitivity specifications only.

From the raw inputs all variables were harmonized to an annual frequency, and all were aligned by the calendar year. Missing observations that resulted from data availability in macroeconomic series were not imputed and were excluded from specifications which require those controls.

\section{Results quantification}\label{results-quantification}

The magnitude of the post-break structural change is quantified by the
coefficients associated with the break terms in the linear regression
model in Equation~\eqref{eq:its-model}, where the parameter $\beta_{2}$ measures the immediate level shift in
the outcome variable at the structural breakpoint (interpreted here as
the estimated impact under the maintained assumption of a stable
pre-intervention trend), while $\beta_{3}$ captures the change in the
slope of the outcome trajectory after the identified structural break.

For any post-break period $t \geq T_{0}$, the estimated post-break
dynamic deviation from the counterfactual is given by Equation~\eqref{eq:dynamic-deviation},

\begin{equation}
\widehat{\tau}_{t} = \widehat{\beta}_{2} + \widehat{\beta}_{3}(t - T_{0})
\label{eq:dynamic-deviation}
\end{equation}
\addequationtolist{Post-break dynamic deviation from the counterfactual}

which represents the deviation of the observed outcome from its
counterfactual path implied by the pre-intervention trend.

The reduced-form parameters are estimated by OLS. Under the maintained
exogeneity assumption, Equation~\eqref{eq:exogeneity} holds.

\section{Results evaluation}\label{results-evaluation}

Consistency is evaluated by examining whether the estimated coefficients
associated with the intervention, \({\widehat{\beta}}_{2}\) and
\({\widehat{\beta}}_{3}\), remain stable in sign, order of magnitude,
and statistical significance across alternative but theoretically
equivalent model specifications.

The robustness of the estimated post-break coefficients to key modelling
assumptions is evaluated by examining their stability across
specifications. The analysis specifically examines sensitivity to the
selection of the structural break date \(T_{0}\), to the
pre-intervention trend break allowance, and to alternate serial
correlation treatments using various HAC specifications.

