Finally, ASFRs at older maternal ages display a consistent and robust adjustment pattern. For women aged 35-39, the pre-break time trend is negative and significant (β = −0.00177; p < 0.01), accompanied by a negative level shift at the structural break (β = −0.057; p < 0.01) and a positive post-break slope coefficient (β = +0.00170; p < 0.01). Female labour force participation exerts a strong positive effect, while youth unemployment is negatively and significantly associated with fertility at these ages. A very similar pattern emerges for women aged 40-44, with highly significant coefficients for the time trend, level shift, and post-break slope, and a robust positive association with female labour force participation. The asymmetry between the two age groups\footnote{The 35-39 group shows partial convergence toward the counterfactual while the 40-44 group does not.} is the central demographic finding used to calibrate the additionality parameter in the economic model.


\section{Logit ITS model}\label{app:model:logit_its}

In order to add to a linear ITS specification found in \autoref{linear-its-model}, we estimate a logit model for a binary indication of low-fertility regime membership. Let $Y_t^{L}$ be a binary value equal to 1 when adjusted TFR falls within the low-fertility regime and 0 otherwise. The logit specification is:

\begin{equation}
\Pr(Y_t^{L}=1 \mid X_t) = \frac{\exp\left(\alpha_0 + \alpha_1 t + \alpha_2 D_t + \alpha_3 (t-T_0)D_t + \delta' X_t\right)}
{1+\exp\left(\alpha_0 + \alpha_1 t + \alpha_2 D_t + \alpha_3 (t-T_0)D_t + \delta' X_t\right)}.
\label{eq:logit_its_appendix}
\end{equation}

\subsection{ROC Curve Analysis}\label{app:model:roc}

The area under the receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve (AUC) is 1.00, illustrating extremely high discriminatory ability. Values near one indicate the model's high performance in discriminating between low fertility regime observations and non-low fertility regime ones. In this case, the ROC curve lies entirely above the diagonal benchmark, implying near-perfect separation between the two outcome states.

