Choosing a cut-off value of 50\% seems to offer sensitivity and specificity trade-offs, but to consider with caution. At this threshold, the model correctly characterizes almost all observations associated with the low-fertility regime, with very few false negatives indicating very high sensitivity. However, the model is limited in terms of identifying observations outside of the low-fertility regime, leading to a high false positive rate.

From a technical point of view, these results imply that it is very effective at identifying the low-fertility regime but much less effective at ruling it out. Hence, the extreme shape of the ROC curve and the AUC indicate quasi-complete separation in the data. As a result, classification performance is mainly associated with powerful underlying trends, not nuanced probabilistic discrimination. Thus, it is relevant to interpret the ROC analysis from a qualitative point of view as a clear indication of meaningful regime disparity instead of an accurate metric of predictive efficacy.

AUC = 1.00 gives us some caution: It most likely reflects the induced endogenous construction of the dependent variable with reference to the adjTFR series itself. A model which perfectly separates a binary variable derived from the same continuous series it is explaining is mechanically regaining its own construction. The interpretation is in agreement with Nagelkerke $R^2 = 0.988$ and convergence after 65 iterations. The model's usefulness lies in proving the structural breaks noted by the linear ITS, not being used to measure it independently.

\subsection{Interpretation}\label{app:model:interpretation}

Several covariates exhibit systematic relationships with the probability of entering a low-fertility regime. The most significant are the long-run time trend, the post-break slope, GDP per capita growth, and female labour force participation rates. The evidence for youth unemployment is weaker and less stable, whereas the level-shift coefficient at the structural break (and the constant term) cannot be distinguished from each other directly due to quasi-complete separation in the data, owing to the fact that our dependent variable is sourced from the same adjTFR series used in the linear ITS.

Importantly, the lack of an identifiable level-shift effect at the structural break indicates that the transition to low fertility is not an abrupt one at $T_0$ but points toward a gradual shift in the underlying trajectory of fertility dynamics. This aligns with quasi-complete separation: in the logit specification, the level-shift component cannot be distinct from the intercept. Given the general nature of nonlinear model estimates using relatively short time series, the loss of statistical significance for individual coefficients should be interpreted as a lack of evidence of a meaningful marginal effect, rather than the absence of association.

The estimated time trend shows a strong upward tendency of entering a low-fertility regime during the pre-break period. This is in line with a marked secular decline in fertility prior to 2010. In contrast, the post-break slope exhibits moderation of such a tendency, indicating a slowing down of the overall rate at which low fertility increases after $T_0 = 2010$. This pattern indicates partial stabilisation instead of reversal of long-run trends; a result that is consistent with the positive post-break slope coefficient in the linear ITS.

