\section{Final regression model}\label{app:model:final}

The two modelling approaches produce mutually consistent results. The linear ITS model quantifies the magnitude of the level shift and post-break slope change in fertility indicator levels. The logit specification\footnote{Understood as a qualitative robustness check on the structural break identified by the linear model, rather than an independent source of identification.} corroborates the direction and persistence of the post-2010 fertility regime change by evaluating the probability of observing high-fertility outcomes before and after the break.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.88\textwidth]{media/media/image17.png}
\caption{Decomposition of changes in adjusted total fertility: structural versus macroeconomic components}
\label{fig:decomposition}
\caption*{\footnotesize\textit{Note:} The figure breaks down the fitted adjTFR trajectory into its structural (trend-related and intervention-related) and macroeconomic components within the ITS framework. The solid black line represents the total fitted adjTFR. The dashed purple line represents the structural component, reflecting the underlying time trend and the post-break slope change. The dotted orange line shows the contribution of the macroeconomic covariates (GDP growth, female labour force participation and youth unemployment). The vertical red dashed line marks the structural breakpoint (T₀ = 2010).}
\end{figure}

Prior to 2010, the structural component followed a consistent downward trajectory, reflecting a long-term secular decline in fertility. At the structural break, this downward trajectory intensified, indicating a persistent shift in the fertility regime. In contrast, the macroeconomic component evolved positively for much of the post-2010 period, partially offsetting the structural decline. Overall, the decomposition suggests that the sharp post-2010 drop in fertility was driven primarily by structural factors rather than by contemporaneous macroeconomic fluctuations, with macroeconomic conditions providing partial mitigation of the downward pressure in subsequent years.

Focusing first on adjTFR, which captures the level of fertility, the linear regression estimates indicate a substantial and statistically significant negative level shift at the structural break. The estimated break-level coefficient in 2010 is approximately $-1399.35$ (per 1{,}000 women aged 15--49), declining in absolute magnitude to approximately $-856.57$ by 2022, yet remaining clearly negative throughout the post-break period as the positive post-break slope coefficient only partially offsets the initial level shift. This pattern implies a sustained deviation of observed fertility below its pre-break counterfactual path; a structural shift in the regime rather than a short-lived or transitory shock. Notably, the persistence of this post-break effect for over a decade confirms that the structural break at $T_0 = 2010$ is associated with a lasting change in the fertility regime rather than a merely transitory fluctuation.

The logit specification corroborates this structural interpretation from a probabilistic perspective, functioning here as a qualitative robustness check on the linear ITS finding rather than an independent source of identification. The estimated coefficients imply a lower probability of observing high-fertility adjTFR outcomes after the structural break, which is consistent with the regime shift identified in the linear specification. Substantively, the structural break is associated with both a lower average level of fertility and a reduction in the upper tail of the fertility distribution, decreasing the likelihood of an aggregate fertility rebound in the post-2010 period.

\section{Model assessment}\label{model-assessment}

The logit model successfully converged after 65 iterations, exhibiting
extremely high goodness-of-fit statistics: -2 log likelihood = 1.63, Cox
\& Snell R² = 0.741 and Nagelkerke R² = 0.988.\footnote{Since the ROC
  analysis has already demonstrated quasi-complete separation, the high
  pseudo-R² values should not be taken as evidence of strong explanatory
  power. In microdata logit models, such magnitudes are unusual and
  reflect the near-perfect discrimination between high- and
  low-fertility regimes induced by the covariates. Under quasi-complete
  separation, maximum likelihood estimates remain finite, but they
  converge towards boundary values. This inflates goodness-of-fit
  indicators and in-sample predictive accuracy mechanically. Therefore,
  these results are better understood as evidence of near-deterministic
  classification within the estimation sample than as an indication of
  robust out-of-sample predictive performance.}

