\chapter{Annex II. Decision-Tree Representation of the Elective OC Cost Model}\label{app:decisiontree}

This Annex shows a stylised decision-tree of the economic logic underpinning the publicly funded elective OC programme analysed in the main text. The model, as described in \autoref{assumptions} and expanded in \autoref{parameters}, merges behavioural parameters (uptake and utilisation rates) with clinical outcomes (success rates) to estimate the expected fiscal impact of the policy.

The figure establishes the sequential design within which the frameworks presented in \autoref{building-on-matorras-discussion}: eligibility in the target population, the choice to undergo vitrification, the chance of using stored oocytes again, and the conditional probability of obtaining a live birth. These are key parameters that are used to interpret the empirical results and the estimated cost-effectiveness as presented in \autoref{post-2010-structural-break-dynamics} and in \autoref{fig:cost-sensitivity}.

When the full path is presented graphically in \autoref{fig:annex2_decision_tree}, the annex helps clarify how programme costs are incurred even if oocytes were never ultimately used, as well as the fact that the fiscal break-even condition is jointly determined by behavioural and clinical margins. Consequently, it should be treated as a schematic example of the analytic framework generated in \autoref{budget-impact}, and not as a standalone empirical outcome.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.88\textwidth]{media/media/image8.png}
    \caption[Decision-tree representation of the elective OC cost model]{Decision-tree representation of the cost structure of a publicly funded elective OC programme.}
    \vspace{0.3em}
    
    {\footnotesize \textit{Note:} The figure illustrates a simplified decision tree used to model the expected fiscal cost per live birth under a publicly funded elective OC programme. The baseline scenario assumes an uptake rate of 8\%, a utilisation rate of 8\%, and a clinical success rate of 30\%. Costs include the vitrification procedure (approximately €4,000 per patient) and IVF costs (approximately €2,500 per cycle). NFC refers to the net fiscal contribution per additional birth, estimated in the main text. The fiscal break-even condition is achieved when the expected cost per live birth is less than or equal to the NFC (see \autoref{cost-per-additional-birth} for full derivation).}
    
    \label{fig:annex2_decision_tree}
\end{figure}

